# @@{title}: Manager GUI panel (Qt WebAssembly)

This folder builds the service's panel for the Manager GUI as a Qt for
WebAssembly module. The GUI shows it in a tile of the service's component
(`../ui/<folder>/component.json`, tile kind `wasm`). The panel never talks to
the service itself: it calls the service's gRPC methods through the GUI, which
answers with the component's capabilities. It links Qt Widgets only, with no
gRPC, protobuf or vcpkg.

| File | What it is |
|---|---|
| `panel_spec.h` | the services and RPCs the panel shows (generated from the proto) |
| `ServicePanel.cpp/.h` | the window: one group per RPC, with its request fields, a Call button and the response |
| `ServiceBridge.cpp/.h` | sends calls to the GUI (`window.callMicroservice`) and matches the answers to them |
| `main.cpp` | the window in a scroll area that fills the tile |
| `build_wasm.bat` / `.sh` | build, and install into the GUI when `MM_SERVICES` is set |

## Build

```bat
build_wasm.bat                 :: Release -> build\wasm\@@{target}.js + .wasm
set MM_SERVICES=<gui>\web\services && build_wasm.bat    :: build and install
```

The scripts default to `C:\Qt\6.11.0\wasm_singlethread` (Linux:
`~/Qt/6.11.0/wasm_singlethread`), the host kit next to it, and `C:\emsdk`
(`~/emsdk`). Override them with `QT_WASM_DIR`, `QT_HOST_DIR` and `EMSDK_DIR`.
Each Qt WebAssembly kit needs one Emscripten version: 4.0.7 for Qt 6.11.

## Install

@@{install_layout}

The build goes in `qt/` on purpose: a `.wasm` at the top of the folder would
also be offered as a classic (broker) panel, which cannot reach a gRPC service.

The service declares its folder in Consul as `Meta.gui`:
@@{gui_env}
Check a component from the Manager GUI folder with
`node tools/endo-lint.js web/services/<folder>/component.json`. Expect one
warning: a wasm tile runs its script in the GUI page.

## Make it yours

- **Calls:** `ServiceBridge::call("<package.Service>/<Method>", request,
  reply)`. The request is the proto message as JSON (proto field names), and
  the reply gets the whole response message.
- **Timers:** calls carry the tile's token (`Module.endoToken`), so calls
  from a timer (polling a status, for example) reach this tile even when
  another tile was clicked last.
- **Several components, one build:** the GUI tells the build which proto
  services the component binds (`Module.endoServices`). The panel shows
  only those.
- **Your own window:** replace `ServicePanel` with a Qt Designer `.ui` and
  your own class, and keep calling through `ServiceBridge`.
- **Tile height:** a Qt canvas does not make its tile grow. Adjust
  `minHeight` (px) of the tile in `component.json` to your window.
- **Server-streaming RPCs:** the panel lists them, and the component shows
  their messages in `log` tiles.
